Model a Worker Who Walks Freely
Model a Worker Who Walks Freely
You can model a Worker who walks freely within the plant.
This way the Worker 
  is not forced to walk on the FootPaths, which you inserted between the
WorkerPool and the Workplaces attached to the work station. For this reason he has to walk around
obstacles, which he cannot enter or cross. The obstacles can be machines, safety fences around robots,
paths of automated guided vehicles, or structural factors in the plant, such as columns, stairways, etc.
As you can only set some of the functions of the Worker, who walks freely within the plant, in 3D, select
3D when you create a new model.
To show the difference between a Worker walking on FootPaths and a Worker, who walks freely within
the plant, we use an existing model with FootPaths. In the screenshot below the Worker walks from the
WorkerPool via the inserted FootPaths to the Workplaces, which are assigned to the stations.
We modify this model by inserting a barred area and demonstrate how to:
• Model a Worker Who Walks Freely Within the Model
• Model a Worker Who Walks One-Way
• Model a Worker Who Walks Through Doors
Model a Worker Who Walks Freely
10-472 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model Workers and the Jobs They Do
Model a Worker Who Walks Freely Within the Model
You can model a Worker who walks freely within the model.
Proceed as follows:
• Double-click the WorkerPool in the 3D model and select Travel Mode > Move freely within the area in
the drop-down list.
• Insert an obstacle around which the Worker has to walk when he walks to the Workplaces at which he
performs his job:
◦ Change to the ribbon tab Edit.
◦ Click Barred Area.
Model a Worker Who Walks Freely Within the Model
Plant Simulation Help 10-473
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ Select the graphic group, the Form, the Dimensions and the Material of the barred area. We
selected the following settings:
◦ The inserted barred area, our obstacle for the worker, looks like this:
By default the barred area is an obstacle for the worker. Press the o key on the keyboard to show
all obstacles for the worker in the model. The Worker has to walk around these obstacles, he cannot
cross them.
Model a Worker Who Walks Freely Within the Model
10-474 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

For the Workplace Plant Simulation shows two bounding boxes with a gap in between. This ensures
that the Worker only stays on the middle of the Workplace. The Worker first has to leave the
Workplace before he can change his walking direction toward the next target object.
For the length-oriented objects Plant Simulation approximates the display of the bounding box only
roughly to the curve, i.e., it does not show the curve of the conveyor as a smooth curved line.
For most of the 3D objects you can select if they are an Obstacle for the Worker on the Tab Graphics
or on the Tab Graphic Settings in the dialog Edit 3D Settings. For the built-in material flow objects
the bounding box of the graphic is an obstacle for the worker.
Model a Worker Who Walks Freely Within the Model
Plant Simulation Help 10-475
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Change to the Home ribbon tab, run the simulation and watch how the Worker walks from the
WorkerPool to the Workplaces, how he ignores the FootPaths and walks around our barred area. In
the screenshot below we marked his route in black for clarification purposes.
Back to Model a Worker Who Walks Freely
Model a Worker Who Walks One-Way
You can model a Worker, who can only enter the Workplace from one direction only.
To do so, we insert a Barred Area with the Form > One-sided delimitation into our model.
Model a Worker Who Walks One-Way
10-476 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The Worker then can cross the line on the side with the dashed line but not on the side with the solid line.
Depending on the insertion direction of the barred area, he can walk from left to right, but not from right
to left.
Our finished model looks like this. We used all objects with their default settings.
We inserted a WorkerPool, a Broker, and a Source with an attached Workplace surrounded by a safety
fence. The Worker walks toward the Source, enters the one-way barred area, steps onto the Workplace,
Model a Worker Who Walks One-Way
Plant Simulation Help 10-477
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

picks the produced part up, crosses the second one-way barred area, carries the part to the Workplace
attached to the Station, and places it there.
Then the Worker walks back to the Source to pick up the next part. The barred area on the right is
one-way as well, meaning he cannot walk through it from right to left, which would be the shortest way.
Instead, he has to walk around the safety fence and walk through the one-way barred area on the left to
continue working.
Back to Model a Worker Who Walks Freely
Model a Worker Who Walks Through Doors
You can model a Worker, who has to overcome obstacles in your model, you have to use FootPaths even if
the Worker walks freely within the model.
In the example below we want the Worker to walk through the doors from the break room onto the actual
shop floor and back. For displaying the shop floor in our model we use the layout file of the actual factory.
Model a Worker Who Walks Through Doors
10-478 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The layout file is a graphics file which we added to the scene in 3D as the background. Although the
graphics file contains all of the doors, 3D treats the factory as a single unit, whose graphics is an obstacle
for the Worker, which the Worker cannot enter and cross.
When we display the barred areas, by pressing the o key, we can see this clearly by the continuous red
border.
To allow the Worker to walk through the doors, we laid FootPaths through the doorways. The FootPaths
always have to be a little longer than the depth of the obstacle.
For the FootPaths not the length, which you enter on the tab Attributes is decisive, but the length of the
FootPath in the 3D scene. The distance covered of the Worker is determined by the starting point and the
end point of the FootPath as well as by the anchor points.
Model a Worker Who Walks Through Doors
Plant Simulation Help 10-479
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

If the FootPath includes an incline, the Worker moves slower on the incline than on a flat surface. Plant
Simulation achieves this by extending the imagined distance for the incline segments. When computing
the length of an inclined segment, Plant Simulation triples the height difference.
Compare this example: A segment of a FootPath has a dimension of 1 m in the x-direction and 1 m in the
z-direction, thus actually is 1.414 m long. The imagined dimension however is 1 m in the x-direction and 3
m in z-direction, so that Plant Simulation computes an effective length of 3.162 m.
The Worker always walks on the route, which has the shortest effective length and which he can thus
cover in the shortest time. This can cause the Worker to take an actually longer but flatter route to the
destination instead of a shorter route which contains inclines and gradients.
For computing the effective route length Plant Simulation triples the height difference. The resulting
speeds of the Worker are realistic in general. The speed of the Worker decreases to a third, when the
Worker moves vertically up or down, for example when climbing a ladder. The speed decreases to about
half, when the Worker negotiates an incline of 38 degrees, for example when walking on a stairway.
When we run the simulation, we can watch how the Workers walk through the doors of the break rooms
into the anteroom and on onto the shop floor. On the shop floor itself, the Workers walk freely again.
Model a Worker Who Walks Through Doors
10-480 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model a Worker Who Walks Freely
Model a Worker Who Walks Through Doors
Plant Simulation Help 10-481
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Model a Worker Who Climbs Stairs
In this example we model an overpass to allow Workers to safely cross the conveyors of a production line.
To model the overpass, we insert a set of Stairs, a Mezzanine, and a second set of Stairs. The Worker
climbs the first staircase onto the platform spanning the conveyor and then climbs down the second
staircase to return to the factory floor. The routing mechanism automatically sends the freely walking
Worker on the correct path to this destination, the Workplace attached to the Station in our case.
The finished model looks like this:
Insert and Configure the Mezzanine
First, insert the platform on which the Worker crosses the production line.
• Click the Edit ribbon tab in 3D. Click Edit > Insert Shape > Mezzanine.
• Select the settings for the mezzanine. We selected these settings.
Model a Worker Who Climbs Stairs
10-482 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• The mezzanine looks like this:
Insert and Configure the Staircases
Then, we insert the staircases, which the Worker climbs to get onto and off the overpass.
• Click the Edit ribbon tab in 3D. Click Edit > Insert Shape > Stairs.
• Select the settings for the stairs on which the Worker gets onto the overpass. We selected these settings.
Model a Worker Who Climbs Stairs
Plant Simulation Help 10-483
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Rotate the stairs and move it to the mezzanine until they touch. The model then looks like this:
• Repeat the steps to insert a second set of stairs with the same settings. Move it to the mezzanine until
they touch. The model then looks like this:
Model a Worker Who Climbs Stairs
10-484 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Then remove the section of the mezzanine railing where the stairs connect to them. Otherwise our
Worker cannot use the overpass.
◦ Click the mezzanine with the left mouse button once. Plant Simulation selects the entire graphic of
the mezzanine.
◦ Press the + key until only the part of the railing is selected where the stairs connect to it.
Model a Worker Who Climbs Stairs
Plant Simulation Help 10-485
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Press Del to delete this section of the railing.
• Repeat this for the section at the other end of the mezzanine.
• While modeling we noticed, that out mezzanine is too short. To fix this, we selected it and pressed the
spacebar. We changed the Dimension Y to 4.5 meters.
Note
When you view the Obstacles for the Worker, you will notice that there is a gap of 40 cm between
the obstacles and the objects/graphics. This is because we supposed the width which a Worker needs
to pass through to be 80 cm. As our Workers do not have width as such, you see this distance
between the obstacles instead.
Model a Worker Who Climbs Stairs
