Model a Worker Who Walks to a Workplace Attached to a Store
In this example we model a Worker, who walks freely within the area of the simulation model, who picks
up a part at a Source, walks to the Workplace attached to the Store, and places the part in the Store.
Insert the objects into the simulation model so that they match the screenshot below:
The Source produces the parts, which the Worker picks up at the attached Workplace and carries them to
the Workplace attached to the Store.
• We use the Source with its default settings.
• For the WPSource we selected the Transport Importer when we inserted it. We did not change any
other settings.
• For the Store we selected these settings:
Model a Worker Who Walks to a Workplace Attached to a Store
Plant Simulation Help 10-517
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• For the WPStore we selected the Transport Importer when we inserted it. We use it with its default
settings, namely Pick/Drop at Store > Walk to Workplace.
• We use the Drain and its attached WPDrain with their default settings.
• Connect the Source, the Store, and the Drain.
Model a Worker Who Walks to a Workplace Attached to a Store
10-518 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Run the simulation. You'll see that the Worker picks up a part from the WPSource, walks to the WPStore,
steps onto it, and places the part into the leftmost storage bay in the top row. He then walks back to the
WPSource and repeats the procedure until all storage bays in both rows are filled with a part each.
When he is finished, he returns to the WorkerPool.
As we want the Worker to pick the parts from the storage bays and carry them to the WPDrain, we created
an observer for the read-only attribute Full of the class of our Store. As soon as the Store is full, we will
lock the exit of the Source, make the Worker stay on the WPStore, and carry the parts to the WPDrain.
The observer calls the method named OnFullValueChanged is a user-defined attribute of the Store.
We typed in this source code:
-- param attribute: string, oldValue, newValue: boolean
-- param attribute: string, oldValue: boolean
param newValue: boolean
Source.ExitLocked := true
if newValue
    var p:object := ?.cont
    while p /= VOID
        p.move
        waituntil p.location /= ?
        p := ?.cont
Model a Worker Who Walks to a Workplace Attached to a Store
Plant Simulation Help 10-519
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

    end
end
waituntil ?.Empty
Source.ExitLocked := false
When we run the simulation, we see that the Worker picks the part he placed first into the leftmost storage
bay in the top row of the Store, carries it to the WPDrain, and puts it onto the Drain. He continues doing
this until all storage bays are empty and then he starts filling the Store again with parts, etc.
To show the route/Sankey flows of the Worker, we inserted a SankeyDiagram and specified the Worker on
the tab Objects.
When the Worker walks from the WPSource to the WPStore, we see that he steps onto the WPStore and
drops the parts, and Plant Simulation then automatically places them into the correct storage bay.
Model a Worker Who Walks to a Workplace Attached to a Store
