Set How Many Workers Are Created During Initialization
You can set how many Workers are created during initialization.
At times, you may want to change the Workers to Create of the WorkerPool for the next simulation run.
You can accomplish this with the Init Control of the EventController. Plant Simulation calls the init control
Set How Many Workers Are Created During Initialization
10-542 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

once at the beginning of the simulation run during the init phase before the objects in the simulation
model are initialized and before any init methods are executed.
The finished simulation model to demonstrate this looks like this:
To create the model, proceed like this:
• Insert the required material flow and resource objects.
As we do not use Footpaths on which the Workers walk to the Workplaces, select Beam to workplace.
This way they are teleported from the WorkerPool directly to the Workplaces without using up any time
at all.
Set How Many Workers Are Created During Initialization
Plant Simulation Help 10-543
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Insert a Method into which you enter the source code of the init control. We named our method
myInitControl.
• Double-click the EventController, select Tools > Edit Controls, click 
 , and select the method which
you just inserted as the init control.
Set How Many Workers Are Created During Initialization
10-544 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Insert a DataTable into which you enter the information about the Workers to be created. We named our
DataTable MyWorkersTable. Format the table as shown in the screenshot below.
• Drag the Worker, which you want to create, from the Class Library to the first cell of the column Worker,
and drop it there. We want to create the Workers John and Nellie.
Set How Many Workers Are Created During Initialization
Plant Simulation Help 10-545
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Program how many Workers you want to create. Double-click the init control, myInitControl in our
case, and enter the source code. Our instruction [2,1] := 2 enters 2 into the first cell the column
Amount of the table MyWorkersTable. This creates two instances of our Worker named John. Our
second instruction [2,2] := 1 enters 1 into the second cell of the column Amount. This creates
one instance of our Worker named Nellie. Finally, assign the table MyWorkersTable to the Workers to
Create table of the WorkerPool with the method setWorkersToCreateTable.
MyWorkersTable[2,1] := 2 // enters the desired amount of the worker John
MyWorkersTable[2,2] := 1 // enters the desired amount of the worker Nellie
WorkerPool.setWorkersToCreateTable(MyWorkersTable)
• Click 
  in the EventController to open the List of scheduled events. You’ll see the first event to be
processed is the Init event.
When you click 
 , Plant Simulation processes the event, enters the numbers of the Workers it created
into the column Amount of the Workers to Create table and places the graphics of the Workers onto
the WorkerPool.
